A few of these swaps are wild once you actually look at the numbers. Guava is the biggest surprise here — it's not just "high in vitamin C," it's loaded with it because the edible skin and seeds hold most of the concentration, something an orange's peel-and-toss setup can't match. Swiss chard's magnesium works differently too: it's tied to chlorophyll production in the leaf, so the darker and more mineral-rich the soil, the more it packs in per bite. And lentils beating a burger patty on iron matters more than it seems — plant iron (non-heme) absorbs slower, but pairing lentils with a squeeze of lemon or tomato boosts absorption significantly, something most people never think to do. Everyone's body and needs are different, so listen to yours before overhauling your plate.

